  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1223 PM CDT Sun Sep 6 2026\n\n17Z synopsis shows a large upper level ridge occupying much of the \ncentral parts of the CONUS with a slow moving and persistent 700 mb \nshortwave which has kept clouds and dying showers roughly from Hays \nto Liberal throughout the morning.  These clouds have kept \ntemperatures down however with clearing skies we should see a quick \nwarm up by late afternoon.  At the surface an outflow boundary from \nthe storms is positioned from Pratt to Meade with northerly winds on \nthe backside...we should see the winds turn back to the south as a \n1007 mb low strengthens in eastern Colorado.\n\nTonight another shortwave will develop in eastern Colorado with the \nsouthwest flow aloft and CAMs have shown a few isolated storms \ndeveloping in southeast Colorado late this afternoon into the \nevening. Upper level flow aloft is weak so the general thought \nis the storms will struggle to cross the state line and POPs are\nless than 10% from Syracuse to Elkhart. \n\nMonday we should see a similar weather pattern in the upper levels \nwith southwest flow aloft, a developing 700 mb shortwave in the \nafternoon and a deepening surface low in the Rockies which will help \nto increase the winds out of the south 15-25 mph with higher gusts. \nTemperatures should warm back to near 100 but with the lack of lower \nlevel moisture most heat index values from short term models keep \nthe area under the heat index criteria of 105 so I decided to not \nissue one at this point for tomorrow.  Late in the afternoon storms \nwill develop in eastern Colorado along a weak frontal boundary and \nthe shortwave and move generally into northwest Kansas.  I added \nsmall POPs (20%) from Syracuse to Scott City given that the upper \nlevel winds will be a little stronger and this could push the storms \ninto our far northwest zones.  CAPE values should generally be 1000 \nJ/kg or below so severe weather is not anticipated.\n\nTuesday will probably be the hottest day of the week with\nwidespread 100 degree highs and heat index values pushing 105\nmainly along and east of highway 183. \n\nMidweek a stronger trough in the northern plains should provide \nmomentum for a surge of cooler air to reach into north central and \nnorthwest Kansas by the late afternoon hours on Tuesday.  Another \nshortwave in the southwest flow should be more positioned in Kansas \nthat thunderstorms are forecast to break out along the boundary \nroughly from an Elkhart to Hays line.  At this time we have general \nthunder for the severe forecast but eyeballing the skew-T sounding \nfrom the shorter term models there are CAPE values of 1500-2000 J/kg \nand bulk shear values around 30 kts which would suggest these storms \ncould have some wind/hail potential.  Storms should slowly move \nsouth through the night and then a reinforcing push of colder air on \nWednesday should move the front completely through southwest Kansas. \nNBM trends for Thursday are now showing highs around 90 degrees.\n\nSaturday into Sunday long term ensembles continue the trend of a \nstrong upper level wave moving from northwest Canada into the \nnorthern plains which will provide momentum for cooler air to move \nthrough western Kansas on Saturday.  Sunday's highs are now showing \nto be in the mid to upper 80s.",
